		<item>
			<title>Mohamed Al-Fayed, Sean Penn and Guy Ritchie for Madonna's W.E.</title>
			<link>http://drownedmadonna.com/index/item/108-mohamed-al-fayed-sean-penn-and-guy-ritchie-for-madonnas-we.html</link>
			<guid>http://drownedmadonna.com/index/item/108-mohamed-al-fayed-sean-penn-and-guy-ritchie-for-madonnas-we.html</guid>
			<description><![CDATA[<div class="K2FeedIntroText"><p>In an interesting interview by the <strong>Philippine Daily Enquirer</strong>, <strong>Madonna</strong> talks about her talking with her exes <strong>Guy Ritchie</strong> and <strong>Sean Penn</strong> about her film <strong>W.E.</strong>, and negotiating with <strong>Mohamed Al-Fayed</strong>.</p>
<p> </p>
<p><strong>Madonna</strong> had to get the permission of <strong>Mohamed Al-Fayed</strong> to film at the former home of <strong>King Edward III</strong> and <strong>Wallis Simpson</strong> in the <strong>Bois de Boulogne</strong>, and read some of the letters between the Duke and the Duchess <strong>Al-Fayed</strong> is in possession.</p>
<p>"I wanted to not only film there but also to use his likeness in the film. So I needed to get his permission," <strong>Madonna</strong> says. "I also knew he was in possession of many of the letters between the Duke and the Duchess. So I had many reasons to want to meet him.</p>
<p>"He was extremely generous and forthcoming.  As soon as I came into his office, he opened up books and pages of letters to let me look at. He didn’t let me walk away with any of them. We had several meetings.</p>
<p>"He wanted to see the script. He wanted to know how he was portrayed so I had to give him the scenes that were written about him. And they met his approval (laughing).</p>
<p>"Then he gave me permission. I agreed to help him with the charity that his daughter was in charge of, a school for underprivileged and abused children in England. So it was a tradeoff. I helped him with his daughter’s charity and he let me film in his house. I think that was fair trade."</p>
<p> </p>
<p><strong>Madonna</strong> also told the <strong>Enquirer</strong> of her talks with her former husbands <strong>Sean Penn</strong> and <strong>Guy Ritchie</strong> about her movie <strong>W.E.</strong></p>
<p>"I never showed the script to Sean. I only talked to him about making the film," reveals Madonna. "He’s always been supportive of me as a creative person."</p>
<p>As for <strong>Guy Ritchie</strong>, she tells that when she was writing the script, and probably they still were married, "I did give the screenplay to <strong>Guy</strong> at a certain point. I also shared the concepts, stories and ideas with <strong>Guy</strong> because he was also interested in the story from a historical point of view."</p>
<p>"They never really gave me specific advice about telling this story but <strong>Guy</strong> would give me technical advice about cameras or using digital versus film or one cinematographer being better than another or one sound mixer being better than another," <strong>Madonna</strong> adds.</p>
<p> </p>
<p><strong>Madonna</strong> also talked about the filmmakers who inspired her especially on <strong>W.E.</strong></p>
<p>" I was inspired by <strong>Ingmar Bergman</strong>’s film <strong>“Persona”</strong> because it’s the story of two women who have this symbiotic relationship and, in many ways, they switch positions. By the end of the movie, you’re not sure who’s the crazy one and who isn’t," Madonna confesses. "The nature of their relationship was interesting to me."</p>
<p>"I’m also a really big fan of <strong>Alain Resnais</strong> and his film, <strong>“Last Year at Marienbad.”</strong> I loved his use of camera movement and filming in long tracking shots down hallways and mirrors. That film was cutting edge for its time. It broke a lot of rules about filmmaking," <strong>Madonna</strong> says. "All the actors would show up on the set every day. None of them even had a script but they were so in awe of the director that they were willing to just be there, wait and hear what he wanted them to do. I was fascinated by the fact that the actors were willing to take this risk and that he was willing to take that risk, too. He was a big inspiration."</p></div>]]></description>
