		<item>
			<title>Madonna could re-edit some scenes of W.E. ready by the BFI London Film Festival to make the film be a contender at the Oscars</title>
			<link>http://drownedmadonna.com/index/item/103-madonna-could-re-edit-some-scenes-of-we-ready-by-the-bfi-london-film-festival-to-make-the-film-be-a-contender-at-the-oscars.html</link>
			<guid>http://drownedmadonna.com/index/item/103-madonna-could-re-edit-some-scenes-of-we-ready-by-the-bfi-london-film-festival-to-make-the-film-be-a-contender-at-the-oscars.html</guid>
			<description><![CDATA[<div class="K2FeedIntroText"><p>In a previous news we brough to your attention that, while she was in Italy for the <strong>Venice Film Festival 2011</strong>, in an interview with <strong>Daily Mail</strong>'s <strong>Baz Bamigboye</strong>, <strong>Madonna</strong> revealed that <strong>W.E.</strong> maybe "is still a work in progresse," and that, after the screening  gala at the <strong>TIFF Toronto Film Festival</strong> on September 12, she could  decide to change or add some scene. "I would like to think that the film is finished, but I did watch it  the other night [in Venice] and I thought: 'Um, maybe it needs a bit of  this and a bit of that.'" For instance there is a "sexy" steamy scene or two between <strong>Wally</strong> and a <strong>Russian security guard</strong> that was been cut, as were others,  for movie lenght reasons, and now maybe <strong>Madonna</strong> will change her  mind.</p>
<p> </p>
<p><strong>Daily Mail</strong>'s <strong>Baz Bamigboye</strong> is now reporting that an executive close to <strong>W.E.</strong> told him that <strong>Madonna</strong> is being quietly urged to re-edit some scenes of <strong>W.E.</strong>, as directors are used to doing.  <br />"Now it has been shown at the <strong>Venice and Toronto festivals</strong>, <strong>Madonna</strong> has a brief window of opportunity to decide how to shape her movie to best advantage," reports <strong>Baz</strong>. "If <strong>Madonna</strong> wants <strong>W.E.</strong> to be a contender this awards season, she must act fast and have a new version ready by the <strong>UK premiere</strong> at the <strong>BFI London Film Festival</strong> on October 23."  "We see shots of a lot of corridors, so they can go, and other scenes can be trimmed,’ the executive told <strong>Baz</strong>.</p></div>]]></description>
			<category>Movies</category>
			<pubDate>Fri, 16 Sep 2011 01:29:25 +0000</pubDate>
		</item>
